virglrenderer: tell the guest when its context is lost, log it once

What: a new command VIRGL_CCMD_SET_RESET_STATUS_BUFFER (capset bit
VIRGL_CAP_V2_RESET_STATUS_BUFFER, 1u << 30) names a guest-memory buffer
(VIRGL_BIND_CUSTOM, like query results). The first time a context hits a fatal
error the host writes VIRGL_RESET_STATUS_GUILTY into its first dword and logs one
line ("context N (name) is lost ..., the guest driver was told"). A guest Mesa
with src/app/guest/mesa/mesa-virgl-reset-status.patch reports that through
glGetGraphicsResetStatus (GL_GUILTY_CONTEXT_RESET) and EXT/KHR_robustness, so
ANGLE/Chrome and other robust apps recreate their context. Venus: the ring
thread's fatal path logs once per context; the guest's Mesa ends the app on the
fatal ring status (its fences: virgl-venus-lost-context-fences.patch).
Why: a lost virgl context left the guest app drawing into nothing forever; the
host printed one error per failed command instead of saying what happened.
Validation: the buffer must be a PIPE_BUFFER with VIRGL_BIND_CUSTOM (no GL object,
host-side copy in res->ptr) of at least 4 bytes, else the request itself is a
context error. The host only writes 4 bytes at offset 0, through the checked iov
helper; a detached buffer is not written.
Upstream: upstreamable (protocol addition; the command number and capset bit would
be assigned upstream, ours are placed so that older guests never send them).
Not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-context-loss.c (build time: status 0 while alive, GUILTY
after an illegal surface, the context's draws fail, a second context keeps
working, a GL vertex buffer is refused as status buffer);
tests/graphics/context-loss.sh --expect recover (Chrome) and guest/gl-lost.c.
